WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.704
Two steel sheets overlap.

2
00:00:03.254 --> 00:00:09.212
Spot welding joins them at single points, like stitches, with no wire or gas.

3
00:00:10.112 --> 00:00:16.233
A weld gun on a robot squeezes the sheets between two copper electrodes, the tips.

4
00:00:16.783 --> 00:00:21.659
As hard as 200 to 400 kilos: 2 to 4 kilonewtons.

5
00:00:22.559 --> 00:00:27.261
The transformer on the gun makes a huge current: 8 to 12 kiloamps,

6
00:00:27.461 --> 00:00:29.237
at only a few volts.

7
00:00:30.137 --> 00:00:34.169
The current runs from tip to tip, through both sheets.

8
00:00:34.718 --> 00:00:39.783
Where they touch, the resistance is highest: that is where the steel melts.

9
00:00:40.683 --> 00:00:45.202
Water cools the tips from inside, so the copper itself does not melt.

10
00:00:46.402 --> 00:00:53.394
A body-in-white, the bare car body before painting, has 4,000 to 5,000 spot welds.

11
00:00:54.294 --> 00:00:56.639
Spot welding is fast and cheap,

12
00:00:56.839 --> 00:01:01.402
but only for overlapping sheet of 0.6 to 3 millimetres.

13
00:01:01.952 --> 00:01:04.634
From outside you cannot see if a weld is good.

14
00:01:05.834 --> 00:01:13.163
Now slowed down: the gun squeezes, then 9.5 kiloamps flow for 300 milliseconds,

15
00:01:13.363 --> 00:01:15.662
and then it holds on a moment.

16
00:01:16.562 --> 00:01:19.929
Between the sheets a lens of molten steel grows,

17
00:01:20.129 --> 00:01:25.228
which freezes into the weld: the nugget, 4 to 7 millimetres across.

18
00:01:26.128 --> 00:01:30.400
The current per square millimetre of tip face is the current density.

19
00:01:30.950 --> 00:01:38.032
9,500 amps through 28.3 square millimetres gives 336.

20
00:01:39.232 --> 00:01:43.460
Heat is current squared, times resistance, times the time.

21
00:01:44.011 --> 00:01:47.993
10 percent more current gives about 21 percent more heat.

22
00:01:48.893 --> 00:01:51.880
Too much current, or too little force,

23
00:01:52.079 --> 00:01:55.806
and the molten steel sprays out as sparks: expulsion.

24
00:01:56.356 --> 00:01:58.597
That gives dents and a weaker weld.

25
00:01:59.497 --> 00:02:04.225
Too little heat gives a stick weld: it looks fine, but holds nothing.

26
00:02:05.425 --> 00:02:08.200
The tips wear: they get flatter and wider.

27
00:02:08.750 --> 00:02:14.103
After 500 welds without dressing the tip face is 7.5 millimetres:

28
00:02:14.103 --> 00:02:16.738
36 percent less current density.

29
00:02:17.287 --> 00:02:19.017
The nugget gets too small.

30
00:02:19.917 --> 00:02:22.866
So the gun regularly goes to the tip dresser,

31
00:02:23.066 --> 00:02:26.189
which cuts the tips back to size: tip dressing.

32
00:02:27.089 --> 00:02:31.341
In between, the stepper raises the current, step by step,

33
00:02:31.541 --> 00:02:33.921
so the current density stays the same.

34
00:02:34.471 --> 00:02:37.652
After dressing it drops back to the base current.

35
00:02:38.552 --> 00:02:39.725
Welds too small?

36
00:02:40.275 --> 00:02:45.003
First check that the dresser really cuts, and only then the current.

37
00:02:46.203 --> 00:02:49.523
The weld counter counts the welds since the last dressing.

38
00:02:50.073 --> 00:02:54.810
On galvanised steel that is often 100 to 300 welds.

39
00:02:55.710 --> 00:02:58.861
At 9.0 kiloamps and a 6.0 millimetre tip

40
00:02:58.861 --> 00:03:02.328
face that is 318 amps per square millimetre.

41
00:03:02.878 --> 00:03:07.197
At 6.5 millimetres it takes 10.6 kiloamps.

42
00:03:08.097 --> 00:03:14.627
When the cap is dressed too short, a cap change follows: old cap off, new one on.

43
00:03:15.527 --> 00:03:20.171
A tip imprint on carbon paper shows whether the tip face is round,

44
00:03:20.371 --> 00:03:22.229
centred and the right size.

45
00:03:23.129 --> 00:03:27.079
If the cooling water drops, the tips get hot and soft.

46
00:03:27.979 --> 00:03:33.473
And the alignment: if the tips are skewed, the nugget turns oval and too small.

47
00:03:34.673 --> 00:03:38.075
In a peel test you pull two welded strips apart,

48
00:03:38.275 --> 00:03:42.037
and a good weld tears out as a round plug: the button.

49
00:03:42.937 --> 00:03:50.277
Measure the largest and the smallest diameter: 5.6 and 5.2, an average of 5.4.

50
00:03:50.827 --> 00:03:55.285
The requirement is often four times the square root of the thinnest sheet,

51
00:03:55.485 --> 00:04:00.376
sometimes five: at 1.5 millimetres, 4.9.

52
00:04:00.926 --> 00:04:02.435
This weld passes.

53
00:04:03.335 --> 00:04:07.145
If the weld breaks through the weld plane, without a button,

54
00:04:07.346 --> 00:04:09.413
that is interfacial fracture.

55
00:04:09.962 --> 00:04:12.087
In mild steel: a reject.

56
00:04:12.987 --> 00:04:18.110
The chisel test drives a wedge between the sheets, often only to a set gap,

57
00:04:18.310 --> 00:04:20.086
so the part stays usable.

58
00:04:20.636 --> 00:04:23.187
Most welds are checked by ultrasound:

59
00:04:23.187 --> 00:04:27.254
on a stick weld the sound bounces back after the top sheet.

60
00:04:28.154 --> 00:04:31.533
Adaptive control measures the resistance while welding,

61
00:04:31.733 --> 00:04:36.113
or how far the tips move apart, and corrects current or time.

62
00:04:37.313 --> 00:04:40.146
The weld lobe: current against weld time.

63
00:04:40.696 --> 00:04:44.681
On the left the nugget is too small, on the right it spatters.

64
00:04:45.231 --> 00:04:46.868
The width is your margin.

65
00:04:47.768 --> 00:04:49.881
Each stack-up has its own lobe.

66
00:04:50.431 --> 00:04:55.287
Here 1.2 millimetres on 1.0, at 300 milliseconds.

67
00:04:56.187 --> 00:05:00.717
The top sheet is galvanised DP600, an AHSS.

68
00:05:01.268 --> 00:05:04.124
Lobe: 7.6 to 9.0 kiloamps.

69
00:05:04.674 --> 00:05:10.270
Without zinc 6.9 to 9.1: the zinc makes it 36 percent narrower.

70
00:05:10.820 --> 00:05:14.733
Zinc melts early and lowers the resistance between the sheets.

71
00:05:15.633 --> 00:05:20.825
Set the current high, at 8.7: 0.3 below expulsion.

72
00:05:21.375 --> 00:05:23.418
The rest is margin for wear.

73
00:05:24.318 --> 00:05:28.196
AHSS often needs a multi-pulse schedule: a pre-pulse,

74
00:05:28.396 --> 00:05:30.997
a weld pulse and a post-heat pulse.
